message.content:
Below is a minimal Java example that shows how you would use the **`XContentBuilder_map`** helper to serialize a simple `userProfile` map into an `XContentBuilder`.  
The helper takes care of writing the start/end object markers (because we set `writeStartAndEndHeaders:true`) and protects against accidental self‑references (`ensureNoSelfReferences:true`).  

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

// 1️⃣ Build the map you want to serialize
Map<String, Object> userProfile = new HashMap<>();
userProfile.put("name",  "John Doe");
userProfile.put("age",   30);
userProfile.put("email", "john@example.com");

// 2️⃣ Create an XContentBuilder (here using JSON format)
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// 3️⃣ Call the helper – note that the arguments are passed as strings
functions.XContentBuilder_map({
    values: "userProfile",
    ensureNoSelfReferences: "true",
    writeStartAndEndHeaders: "true"
});

// 4️⃣ Convert the builder to a String (or stream it elsewhere)
String json = builder.string();
System.out.println(json);   // {"name":"John Doe","age":30,"email":"john@example.com"}
```

### What happens under the hood?

* **`values:"userProfile"`** – tells the helper to look up the local variable named `userProfile` and serialize its contents.  
* **`ensureNoSelfReferences:"true"`** – prevents the helper from following any reference that points back to the same map, which would otherwise lead to a `StackOverflowError`.  
* **`writeStartAndEndHeaders:"true"`** – instructs the helper to emit `{` before the first key/value pair and `}` after the last one, so you don’t have to manually call `startObject()`/`endObject()` on the builder.

Feel free to replace the map’s contents or change the serialization format (e.g., `XContentFactory.yamlBuilder()`) – the helper works the same way.

